00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:03.303 align:start size:81% position:19%
- I really just
worry about Google

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:05.438
being able to swing an election.

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:08.375 align:start size:97% position:3%
At least when Facebook
rips the Hunter Biden story

00:00:08.375 --> 00:00:11.177
off the internet two or three
weeks before the election.

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:12.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Mm-hmm.
- Which is complete crap,

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:14.414
and they shouldn't
be allowed to do it,

00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:16.483 align:start size:84% position:16%
but when they did
that, people noticed,

00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:19.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you have the Streisand
effect sort of thing,

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:20.253
where it's this discussion.

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:21.588 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Right.
- We can talk about it

00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:23.590 align:start size:97% position:3%
and at least the
censorship was transparent,

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:27.327 align:start position:0%
but with Google, they can change
their search algorithms to,

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:28.862
if they're subtle enough,

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.530
I think you could change
it a meaningful amount,

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.165 align:start size:88% position:13%
suppress this
information about Trump,

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:34.100 align:start size:88% position:13%
and boost this
information about Biden,

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.969
and all of a sudden,

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:38.438
do you really live in
a free country anymore?

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:41.841
(dramatic ambient music)

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:47.547 line:0%
- All right, we are in studio,

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:51.451 line:0%
and joining me today is
the COO of Thiel Capital,

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:53.620
the co-author of, "Zero to One,"

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.923 align:start size:97% position:3%
which I keep on the set
normally, right over there.

00:00:56.923 --> 00:00:58.324
And the next Senator

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:01.094
from the great state of
Arizona, Blake Masters!

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:03.029 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah.
- Welcome to the Rubin Report.

00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:04.364
- Thank you, it's
great to be here.

00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:05.632
- I'm glad to have you man,

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:06.766
I've wanted to do
this for a while.

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:09.402 align:start size:97% position:3%
We wanted to do it in person.
- Yes.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.704
- As I said, you are the
co-author of that book,

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:13.006
which I think was really

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:15.208 align:start size:88% position:13%
one of the sort of
formative things for me

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:18.611 align:start size:97% position:3%
to get involved in the tech
world, and all of that.

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:20.113
And I know that's
obviously something

00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:22.549 align:start size:97% position:3%
that interests you a lot,
so why don't we start there?

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:24.951 align:start size:84% position:16%
How'd you start
working with Thiel,

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:26.820
and getting some of
these ideas across

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.655
about making new things?

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:31.424 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- Yeah well, I was a student
at Stanford Law School,

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:34.461 line:0%
and I remember my friend
said, "Hey, Peter Thiel,

00:01:34.461 --> 00:01:36.963 align:start position:0%
you know, the founder of
PayPal, was gonna teach a class

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:38.231
and so we should
take that class."

00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:39.933
And I didn't know that
much about Peter then,

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:42.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
I knew he was
libertarian, businessman,

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:45.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
billionaire, founded
PayPal, that seemed cool.

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:46.940
- Good recipe.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.074
- So he was into seasteading,

00:01:48.074 --> 00:01:49.042 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:50.310
- This idea that you should

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.344
maybe think about how
to start new countries.

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:52.846
- We should get

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:54.114
back on that idea actually.
- We'll talk about that.

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:55.682 align:start size:84% position:16%
So he seemed like a
cool guy and you know,

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:57.517
what an opportunity to
take a class from someone

00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:59.285
who wasn't just a professor,

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:00.954
you know, actually done
something in the world.

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:02.489
So I took,

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:04.624
hey, I took that class and
sort of befriended Peter.

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:06.626
This is my second
year in law school,

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:09.329
went to go intern for him
at his venture capital fund

00:02:09.329 --> 00:02:12.031
that summer, and then he came
back taught another class,

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:13.433
my last year in law school,

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:15.568
I took really good
notes on that class.

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:18.138
I just basically typed
down everything he said,

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:19.672
'cause I liked the first class,

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:20.940
but I can't tell you
too much about it,

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:21.908
'cause it's all lost to memory,

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:23.176
right.
- Right.

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:25.011
- I wasn't like a big
note taker in college,

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:26.746
'cause who cares?

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:28.047 align:start size:84% position:16%
But the second time
I was prepared,

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:29.716 align:start size:84% position:16%
so I wrote down
everything Peter said,

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:31.818
cleaned it up, posted it online.

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:34.420
And these notes became
kind of like a phenomenon

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.388 align:start size:81% position:19%
in Silicon Valley.
- Uh.

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:36.890
- This was like 2012.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:39.058
'Cause I think it
was the first time

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:41.661 align:start position:0%
you could actually kind of
collect all of Peter's thoughts

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:43.563 align:start size:97% position:3%
on building a startup, that's
what the class was about,

00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:45.198 align:start size:84% position:16%
startups and
technology companies

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:48.067
and how to do new things
in a stagnant society.

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:49.369
And so I think people

00:02:49.369 --> 00:02:50.670
who were interested
in Peter at the time

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.071
and his stock was
sort of rising,

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:54.574
like Facebook was
just about to IPO,

00:02:54.574 --> 00:02:57.377 align:start size:97% position:3%
you could piece together
things from like interviews,

00:02:57.377 --> 00:03:00.613
print interviews, YouTube, but
there was no one set of like

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:02.749
here's Peter Thiel's thoughts
on how to start a startup.

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:06.186 align:start size:97% position:3%
And all of a sudden me is
this random Stanford student,

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:07.420
was publishing this online.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.222
And so Silicon valley,
a couple 1000 people

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:12.258 align:start size:94% position:6%
just started following
week over week these notes

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:13.893 align:start size:81% position:19%
and became this
really successful.

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.461 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Oh, so you were
doing it in real time?

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:16.296 align:start size:97% position:3%
You were in the class.
- I was doing it in real time.

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:17.597
- Putting the notes up

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:18.998 align:start size:94% position:6%
and people were following.
- Real time.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:20.233 align:start position:0%
- So basically they were
getting the education for free.

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:21.100
- That's right.
- Along side you,

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.035
yeah.
- Yeah.

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:23.236
Which is really interesting

00:03:23.236 --> 00:03:24.437 align:start size:81% position:19%
'cause it was this
awesome class,

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:25.672 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you know, I
think we ultimately,

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:26.839
we turned it into a
very successful book,

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:28.308 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I think it
would've just died,

00:03:28.308 --> 00:03:30.510
like all the material and
like Peter worked hard

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.679 align:start size:88% position:13%
to organize all the
thinking for the class,

00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:34.480
but if it's not recorded,

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.183 align:start size:94% position:6%
if it doesn't actually
get outside the classroom,

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:38.618
it just dies there.

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:39.953
And that would've
been a big shame.

00:03:39.953 --> 00:03:41.221
- Yeah.

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:42.455
So the basic premise
of the book is that

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:45.491 align:start size:88% position:13%
the next Facebook is not
going to be Facebook,

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:48.628 align:start position:0%
to go from zero to one, you
have to do something fully new.

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:50.163
- Right.

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:54.467
- Does that feel more sort of
relevant now than it did even,

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:57.837 align:start size:97% position:3%
eight, 10 years ago because
of the sort of upheaval

00:03:57.837 --> 00:03:59.739
and weird spot that
we're in right now?

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:01.007
- It does.

00:04:01.007 --> 00:04:02.742
I think we desperately
do need new technology

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:04.677 align:start size:84% position:16%
to sort of take things
to the next level.

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:07.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
We have a remarkably
stagnant society.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.115
I think people get distracted,

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:10.750
like the iPhone 13
Pro just came out.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.986
It's like, congratulations,
the camera's much better.

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:13.987
And that's cool.

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:15.221
- Wait, is a half inch bigger

00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:16.623 align:start size:97% position:3%
or smaller?
